eady doing  in the world and that god takes maybe  some of the  felt pressure off of me but it also puts  pressure on me to like continue to  seek out that space in that area um and  so i think  and i also think that that i as a  psychiatrist i can often get in the mode  of fixing like  patients presenting me with a problem i  got to fix them and i think as  the our faith gives us a different kind  of frame that we  are not machines that need to be fixed  but we are those who are on a journey  i from god to god and this this  life is a kind of journey uh through  life to god and we walk  we ourselves are on a journey and anyone  whom we  try to help or to minister to or with is  also on a journey so  we walk as wayfarers alongside others on  a journey and so we can ask ourselves  like what's needed right now  for the journey and that makes it very  practical because what's needed  might be a medication what's needed  might be a course of ect  or tms or what's needed might be a 12  session course of psychotherapy or  what's needed might be  a men's group or what's needed might be  a meal or to get out of an abusive  relationship  and so i think i think that that being a  christian gives me permission to think  broadly about those kinds of things and  to and and hope that uh  that this work didn't start with me or  us or any of us  on this uh episode it doesn't end with  us but it's going on and we get a chance  to participate in that  and that gives me hope and then just to  see witnesses like pastor mike and dr  greene  and others in other places doing really  creative work i look at that and i think  man this is so deeply needed and uh to  be able just to see that unfold and to  celebrate it is something that i think  is just really profoundly encouraging  wow that's so well said and i just love  that approach too and the framework  of really just looking at things  holistically and bringing  all aspects and all tools to the table  so definitely appreciate you  sharing that and i know you're being  humble but i know you're deeply involved  in that work in so many ways as well  so deeply appreciate for the way that  you're doing that and the way that  you're impacting  the next generation of folks who will be  doing this both  as clinicians as psychiatrists as  pastors and teachers so i also just  wanted to acknowledge that and  make sure that yeah that's acknowledged  and known to our audience as well  thank you duterte and all of us i think  are in that role in  on this on this call so thank you  dr green what about you you know even  that you know that juxtaposition goes  back to what you mentioned at the  beginning about  even your own personal challenges in the  midst of the hope  that you have as a christian and the  hope that you're providing in your work  how do you  continue to think about that going  forward so  um you know dr kinghorn said it so  so perfectly too um i think about how do  we do this  um work and how we call to do this work  and we're always called to do it in  relationship  right and so that's what really is at  the heart of what we're trying to do and  therapy  and groups on this healing journey is  that i'm walking alongside you in this  relationship on this healing journey  and you know how can i support you um on  this and how do i learn right because  you know i tell folks all the time when  they come here and they you know dr  kinghorn mentioned  you know we want to fix but also our  clients or our innovators expect  expect us to fix it right um and so i  also say to people you're the expert  on your own life right i'm here to  listen  i'm here to maybe pick out some things  that maybe you didn't you weren't aware  of  or um to challenge you in new and  different ways or expand your thinking  but you're the expert on that and and  we're both learning in this space  together  um and so i think that's an important um  lens to have and working with with  people who are on this journey to  healing  um and then also just continually  thinking about you know how do i  personally  intentionally cultivate spaces of joy  what does that look like for me on a  daily and consistent basis  right and i don't sometimes i don't do  it every single day um  right but reminding myself to be  intentional about it uh reminding the  people around me to be intentional about  it  um and holding myself accountable right  yeah that's so well said i love that  that relational piece and  holding ourselves accountable to joy i  mean i think that goes that goes such a  long way  pastor mike to wrap up there's so much  that's been said here  um i guess not even just hope for  yourself but you know if you'd be able  to encourage our listeners too in ways  to continue to find hope because i'm  sure there are those who are listening  who are struggling and who are trying to  find a way out any any uh  where did you want to speak to them  directly yeah there's a  a many years ago a line i heard i  read by late um howard thurman who has  served as one of my own spiritual and  theological mentors along my journey as  a believer and  as a pastor and he was frequent to say  that the contradictions of life are not  final  and i think in these past 18 months  we've seen  many harsh realities that have sought  to um in some places assault life  undermine life  and have given birth birth to many um  many realities that many of us did not  know were even possibilities for us  meaning depressive moments  moments filled with anxiety that this  pandemic brought to the forefront  but i think it's always a good reminder  to tell people  for me personally that those  contradictions are not final they do not  hold the final word they are not  definitive on our lives  that we have not only power through god  but transcendent power to overcome  and see our way through these kinds of  seasons  one of our pastors sermon last sunday  and she focused  on psalm 23 and the particular verse  when  the psalmist says david in particular  you know yea though i walk through the  valley of the shadow of  death i will fear no evil but thou art  with me  that that confidence that the writer  exhibits that  that um audacity  is shaped by an experience with god for  thou  art with me and i think at the end of  the day for believers  who are finding themselves dealing with  mental health challenges we cannot  forget  the presence of god because it is  through god i think that resources  emerges through god that opportunities  come to the forefront and it is also  through god  that new and innovative pathways towards  healing and  wholeness become visible so those  contradictions are not  final the best is all is yet to come  and it does not appear what we shall be  when we trust that we're being led  and guided and directed by a god who at  the end of the day  wants to see us to be the best versions  of ourselves  wow so powerful so well said  amen well  i'm just in awe of just you know  everything that's that we've gone  through and even  what's happened this last hour during  our conversation and you know what's  been happening over the years  at the hope center and grateful grateful  for uh  the trust that you all have put in god  and one another to be able to  walk in relationship like dr green said  in a lot of ways and walking  relationship with those in your  community i think that's  it goes a long way and i'm sure that  it's uh it's important  to the community and acknowledged even  in times when it's challenging so  that yeah that gives me hope just even  hearing hearing this story  today and these stories that you all  shared so thank you so much pastor mike  dr green dr king horn for jumping on the  adi hour podcast i know this is going to  be one of those that people are going to  be able to go back and listen to and  i appreciate the way you spoken so  poignantly to our listeners  as well and been so real and honest at  the same time  well thank you for having me and thanks  for having us this has been great and  i'm  i'm grateful the opportunity just to  share and be part of this conversation  thank you so much  well great being with all of you and for  all of our listeners stay tuned for some  upcoming episodes of the addy hour  continue just like we did today to  really dig deep into some of these  topics and  really have real and honest  conversations i think can empower us and  encourage us all  so until next time  you

